WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.370
JAMES BROOK: I'll start
with a quick recap

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.610
where we are today with
containers, and Kubernetes,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.930
and Google Container Engine.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.930
Oh, something went wrong.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:19.707
Well, actually this isn't
a real outage luckily.

00:00:19.707 --> 00:00:21.540
But for the software
developers in the room,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.350
I have some good news for you.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:27.510
It's not usually your code that
is the main cause of outages.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.455
It's actually the way it's
configured, operated, deployed.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:37.530
So, operating
software is difficult.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.870
We have all of these
things to think about.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.310
So, how do we
deploy our software?

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.880
How do we deal with
continuous integration?

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.630
How do we deal with scaling?

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.510
How do we deal with
monitoring without fear?

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.400
How do our services
discover each other

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.680
so that they can communicate?

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.110
Well, let's see if containers
have some of the answers.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:06.340
Containers give us a consistent
portable packaging format.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.570
So we have somewhere where we
can wrap up our application

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.260
code and our
dependencies together.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.910
It's immutable.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:15.840
It can be versioned.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.270
We can stash it in a
container registry.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.690
It's a really handy artifact
that makes it easy to deploy.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.790
And most importantly,
it can run anywhere.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:28.200
So Docker has become the
de facto container runtime,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.820
and it will run on
pretty much any OS.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.190
We get a lightweight
isolation, which

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.770
means we can disentangle
our application

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.770
processes from each other.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.420
They won't stomp on each
other's application code

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.320
and dependencies.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.990
Libraries can be
managed independently,

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.540
application by application.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.510
And we can set restrictions
on how much CPU and memory

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.310
each of our applications
get, to stop them

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.380
from competing with each other.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.880
And this is all
really lightweight.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.500
So unlike a VMware,
you might have

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.500
a hypervisor and a guest
OS, these restrictions

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.360
are enforced by
the kernel, which

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.194
means they can start up
and stop really quickly.

00:02:06.194 --> 00:02:07.860
And they're kind of
transient processes,

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.820
aside from any data
they might store.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.350
And also we have a
standard control plane.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.200
So no matter what we're running
and where we're running it,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.380
we can stop it, we can
start it in the same way.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.250
We have hooks into monitoring.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.125
But does that get
all the way there?

00:02:27.125 --> 00:02:29.500
I think we still have to think
about all of these things.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.470
So, which virtual machine is
our container going to run on?

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.650
How are we going to replicate
it for fault tolerance?

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.810
How will we know
if it's healthy?

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.970
How will we handle
incoming network traffic,

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.215
load balancing, and
things like that?

00:02:44.215 --> 00:02:46.590
How are we going to wire up
our applications so that they

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.229
can speak to each other?

00:02:48.229 --> 00:02:49.770
And then there's
microservices, which

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:53.520
means if you are decomposing
your monolith or your larger

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.549
service into lots
of little services,

00:02:55.549 --> 00:02:57.090
you have all the
more to think about,

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.965
and you have all of
these extra communication

00:02:58.965 --> 00:03:00.650
paths that suddenly appear.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:04.930
So you have amazing complexities
potentially to deal with there.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.230
Well, that takes
us to Kubernetes.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.320
And I think it has answers
to these questions for us.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.920
So at its root, Kubernetes
allows us to schedule and run

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.400
containers on a fleet
of physical machines

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.640
or virtual machines.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.660
And it allows us to cut the
cord with the physical world.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.410
So, we don't have to
think anymore about where

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.300
our applications are running.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.310
Instead we can
think about things

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.459
in terms of an application or
even a service-centric view

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:33.000
of the world.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:39.770
Kubernetes was initially
developed inside Google.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.540
I think we first announced
it about three years ago.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.590
And it went 1.0
about two years ago.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:50.390
And at that time when it went
1.0, over a quarter of the code

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.870
had come from outside
of Google, which

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:56.871
shows the larger community was
involved from the beginning.

00:03:56.871 --> 00:03:59.120
And in subsequent releases,
more than half of the code

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.680
has come from the
wider community.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.071
So I think you can
see that community

00:04:04.071 --> 00:04:05.570
and the general
software development

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.360
community and operations,
is now the power that's

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.890
driving forward the state of
the art with this framework.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:13.140
And the community is enormous.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.931
I think there was a
"Tech Republic" article

00:04:14.931 --> 00:04:17.230
while ago that showed that
the Kubernetes community is

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:20.420
something like five times
larger than the nearest

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:23.240
similar community.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:26.510
So Google has now
seven apps, I think,

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:31.070
which operate with more
than 1 billion active users.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.560
At Google, we believe
that running applications

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.250
in containers is the
right place to do it.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:40.119
And we've been doing it
ourselves for 12 years.

00:04:40.119 --> 00:04:41.660
And I think you can
think pretty much

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.910
any one of those
services that's serving

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:49.040
those users, maps whatever is
running inside a container.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.250
Now all of this
story with Kubernetes

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:55.880
doesn't mean that your ops team
is going to be out of a job.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.150
The Google ops team, the core
ops teams continue to grow.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:00.920
But the number of
containers in our fleet

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.840
has grown over 10 times faster.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.400
So to tell us a bit about
Kubernetes in the wild,

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.860
I'm really excited to invite
our friend from Excite

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:10.790
to the stage.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.540
So please give Hygo
a round of applause.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:14.780
And he's going to tell us.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.180
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:22.026 --> 00:05:23.150
HYGO REINALDO: Hello, guys.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.460
Thank you, James.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.630
Thank you all for coming.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.540
It's great to be
here today to talk

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:34.470
a bit about Xite,
Kubernetes, and Google Cloud

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:35.980
Platform with UI.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:41.030
For starting, what
we do at Xite?

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:46.160
Well, Xite, it's personalized
music and television channel.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:52.280
You could see it as Spotify
or Netflix for music videos.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:57.200
And our application, it runs
on top of a linear channel.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.290
And based on what
our user decides,

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:04.800
like keep, skip,
or like music; we

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.890
keep doing indications for the
recommendations in real time.

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:14.360
And we have now more than 100K
music videos in our database

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.760
and this amount
only grows each day.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.190
This is how the
application looks like.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.100
On your TV, you can
have different kind

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.470
of channels like
just rock music,

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:32.850
or you can really
personalize it.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.970
And depending on
the actions, this

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:42.080
is what it looks like on your
TV when a user likes a video.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:47.600
This is what it looks like
when he or she skips it.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:51.530
Of course, you can watch
straight away on your TV.

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:56.105
And based on the actions
that the user just took,

00:06:56.105 --> 00:07:01.060
our algorithm improves the video
recommendation in real time.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.980
As you can see, now we are
improving that channel.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:09.290
So it's actually a very cool
thing to run and to maintain.

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.050
And if you want to
test that by yourself,

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:16.940
we invite you all to go to
Ziggo if you have channel 25.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:20.110
This is nice.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:24.250
Now let's go to the
cool part, coolest.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:28.860
When I first started at at Xite,
I found this infrastructure--

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:33.920
running on premises and
encoding a video on Mac Minis.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.790
As you can imagine, a lot of
things could go wrong there.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:39.760
And it did.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:43.600
So we had all kind of
outages, like crashing

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:48.430
servers, limited storage
capacity, power--

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:51.551
electricity power, sometimes
we had electric power

00:07:51.551 --> 00:07:53.050
out in our building
and then we need

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:55.990
to send the entire
music department home,

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:58.310
because they could not work.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:00.550
And of course one
of the main problems

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:06.460
also, the solution on premises
was not scalable at all.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:08.170
If we need to
encode more videos,

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:10.030
then we need to
add more Mac Minis

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.230
and keep maintaining these.

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:16.070
So this was not good.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:19.090
So the first thing that came
to my mind when I arrived

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:22.660
was, not how I would
keep any of these.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:25.140
We are going to
rebuild everything.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:30.860
And about this new platform
that we would start,

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:34.490
well I knew three things
for sure about it.

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:39.309
First of all, it had
to be cloud agnostic.

00:08:39.309 --> 00:08:42.909
Because at Xite, it is
very important for us

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:47.680
to be sure that our
users, they have

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:50.430
been provided with the
best service possible

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.960
we can offer to them.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.780
And we do believe that
to be able to choose

00:08:55.780 --> 00:09:00.010
which cloud platform we are
going to run all solutions,

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:03.740
its an essential
part of this process.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:05.830
So the platform, the
solution, the new one

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:07.960
had to be cloud agnostic.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:11.950
And of course, open source
community had to be behind it.

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:15.940
Because I do know-- we do know
how important the open source

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:18.940
community is for any project.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:24.640
And the great news James just
gave us that now more than 50%

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:29.780
of the code of Kubernetes,
they came from the community.

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:34.120
And another important factor
about this new platform

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:38.770
was that I want it to
be fast and efficient.

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:43.790
Fast at the point that we
could reply all API requests

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:48.340
in like small milliseconds,
and efficient at the point

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:54.260
that we can encode 4K videos,
cheap in an affordable way.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:57.380
So after analyzing these
three conditions, these three

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.400
main conditions, it was
clear that Kubernetes

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.440
would be a great choice for us.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:03.440
And we went for it.

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:11.150
After we decided
going for Kubernetes,

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:14.710
why to go for Google
Container Engine?

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:16.870
If you don't know,
Google Container Engine

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:20.980
is the Google solution
for hosting containers.

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:24.430
Well, so why going for it and
not going to any other cloud

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:25.210
provider?

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:29.330
As you know, Kubernetes can run
in multiple different places.

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:32.350
Well, the first
reason why we decided

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:36.100
going for Google
Container Engine

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:40.682
was the fact that it's already
itself a Kubernetes cluster.

00:10:40.682 --> 00:10:44.140
Which means the Container
Engine itself is already

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.090
running inside Kubernetes.

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:47.920
And this a pretty
cool thing, and it

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:52.450
also proves that the
technology works.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.300
After that, another
important point

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:59.590
on choosing Google
Container Engine platform

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:05.890
was the fact that their Google
key manager, is the Kubernetes

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.440
master for you.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:11.390
And this saves a
long amount of time.

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:15.040
Instead of managing the
master, updating it,

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:17.520
I just left this job to Google.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.030
And I keep focused on
making the cluster better.

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:24.660
And this is like a
huge, huge advantage.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.960
And another thing that we went
for Google Container Engine

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.700
is the cost benefit.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:34.200
They have all kind of discounts
if you keep your server

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:38.065
running for the full month,
all the weeks of-- all

00:11:38.065 --> 00:11:40.200
the days of the month,
you get immediately

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:43.720
a sustained use discount
on your VM, which

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:47.900
can be around 30% of its price.

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:51.320
So it's actually a very
good cost efficient.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.900
So we decided to go
for Kubernetes, then

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:55.900
Google Container Engine.

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:01.130
And this was a good choice.

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:03.500
So I will talk to
you now how we're

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:07.040
doing encoding videos on
Kubernetes and Google Container

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:08.600
Engine.

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:12.950
Actually our encoding
process, it's very efficient.

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:16.400
Every time we need to
encode a new video,

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:20.120
Kubernetes starts a
POD, encoding POD,

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:23.150
on a preemptable VM, 32 vcpus.

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:26.960
Disapprove And the
only job of this POD

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:29.860
is to encode that
specific video.

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:33.770
When it's done, Kubernetes
will automatically

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:39.080
destroy the POD and
also the preemptable VM.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:43.100
If you don't know what a
preemptable VM is, well,

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:46.400
a good way to
explain, this is more

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:51.380
like an access of
computing resources

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:54.770
on that specific
Google data center.

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:58.230
So Google is not
using at that moment,

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:03.300
so Google offers you to
have a VM as a preemptable.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:07.800
Which means that this VM is
going to run up to 24 hours.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:11.750
However, it can be destroyed
by end-time by Google,

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:17.840
with just 30 seconds notice
if this access of computing

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:23.420
resources is needed somewhere
else in the data center.

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:30.200
However, they can cost up to
80% cheaper than a regular VM.

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:32.990
So its actually a good deal.

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:38.020
You can see a preemptable
VM as a beautiful butterfly.

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:43.940
Its going to arrive to your life
fast, make it better, bright,

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:47.240
and suddenly this is out.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:51.920
So your application also
has to be able to handle it,

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:54.580
in case of a resilient
problem which

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:58.620
happens because the preemptable
machine was destroyed.

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:02.540
However, this works
pretty good for us.

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:06.670
And this is one proof that the
integration between Kubernetes

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:11.380
and Google Cloud
Platform works great.

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:17.660
We use Kubernetes node selector
to define on which node pool

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:21.950
that specific POD should start.

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:27.260
A node pool, it's a Google
Container Compute Engine

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:28.530
individually.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:32.760
It allows you to deploy several
different group of machines

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:35.610
that contain the
same hardware on it.

00:14:35.610 --> 00:14:37.470
And it's a very nice feature.

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:41.360
Because in our case,
we can have a node pool

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:48.270
with 32 vcpus preemptable VMs
just for encoding PODs there.

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:51.060
And then on the same
Kubernetes cluster,

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:56.340
we can have a different node
pool with a 4-CPU machine

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:59.070
only for running our API POD.

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:01.170
So this integration
is very powerful.

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:06.180
Because you can use the right
hardware for the right job.

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:08.265
And this, in the
end it saves money.

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:15.780
Another cool thing
we use on Kubernetes

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:21.190
is, of course, the Kubernetes
horizontal pod autoscaling.

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:23.880
This means that
Kubernetes has the ability

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:27.780
to increase and decrease
the number of pods

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:31.110
automatically when it's needed.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:34.620
In our case, our
API in the morning

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:36.330
we have less people using it.

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:39.810
So Kubernetes, it
automatically keeps

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:44.860
the amount of pods running
at the minimal level.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:47.430
However, in the
afternoon, the evening,

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:51.420
when we have more people
watching our channel--

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:53.680
more traffic
arriving, Kubernetes

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:58.030
can detect it, and automatically
increase the amount of pods.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:02.450
So our platform is ready
for this new data arriving,

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:03.970
for this new traffic.

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:08.460
And we can still keep
replying in the prime.

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:12.270
As soon as Kubernetes
realizes these resources are

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:15.210
no longer needed, it
will automatically

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:17.760
decrease your platform down.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.100
So this also saves
money for you.

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:23.670
Because you don't need to
run more resources than what

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:25.470
is really needed on that moment.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:32.980
Another big problem we
had was about the storage.

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:35.920
And the Google Nearline
storage, it really

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.830
solves all the problems we had.

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:42.400
It's a great speed cost benefit.

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:46.335
You can retrieve data faster
in like around one second.

00:16:46.335 --> 00:16:52.150
The cost is only around 10
euros per month for a terabyte.

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:55.630
And this is really midfield
between regular storage

00:16:55.630 --> 00:17:00.130
and a disk attached straight
away to your machine.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:03.370
Because it works
fast, we can even

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:08.020
play some previews of
4K video to our OC,

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:10.150
straight away from the storage.

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:12.849
When we started, I didn't
imagine this could be possible.

00:17:12.849 --> 00:17:18.720
But it works pretty
good, actually.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:24.480
So the main question, $1 million
question, was it all worth it?

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.300
Yes, it was.

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:31.260
If your company is thinking
about migrating to Kubernetes,

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:34.140
you should start it right now.

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:36.390
It has great
benefits, of course,

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:38.520
a bit of work to get it done.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:45.090
And Google Cloud Platform
really helps you to deploy it.

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:48.090
So now, right now we have
our main applications running

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.700
already on Kubernetes,
no longer we need

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:53.010
this in-house infrastructure.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:54.900
Thanks, god.

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:59.340
And we can encode
machines with preemptable

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.830
very fast and efficient for us.

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:03.540
It's cheap.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:06.960
Actually it's 15 times faster
now, the encoding process,

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.930
than what it was in-house.

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:13.920
And the preemptables make
it really affordable.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:16.770
And the Nearline storage
at Google, really fixed

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:21.120
all our problems related
to storage capacity,

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:23.200
and electricity outage.

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:26.550
We don't have anything of
that anymore, of course.

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:30.720
And the most important
aspect, reliability.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:33.460
We can't trust, we can
rely more on our software

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:34.590
that it's going to work.

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:38.130
Because we have
no longer hardware

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:42.090
issues or electricity issues.

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:43.830
It works way better than before.

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:50.590
What's next for us at Xite?

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:53.750
Well, Xite is growing very fast.

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:56.380
We're going to be probably
in 25 different countries

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:58.870
in the next two or three years.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:01.000
So of course I want to
federate everything.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:03.760
I want all my clusters
that I'm going

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:08.140
to release on different zones
around the world to connect,

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:10.900
to integrate, to
put my customers

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:13.990
to talk to each
other, if needed.

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:17.310
And another thing that we are
missing a bit on Kubernetes,

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:20.080
and I have good news
that we're going

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:25.600
to have before in a bit
from James, it's Istio.

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:28.460
It's a servicing mesh system
that we're going to need.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:31.330
Matt talked a bit about it.

00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:33.930
And I'm looking forward
to implementing it.

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:37.630
It's important for us at Xite
to keep using top technologies.

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:42.740
And Istio I do believe is
the next new thing coming.

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:44.770
And this will be a
real game changer

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:46.250
for Kubernetes clusters.

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:52.070
Well, if you have any
questions, this was it for me.

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:53.120
I will be around.

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:56.780
It will be great to talk to
you about Xite, Kubernetes,

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:59.870
or anything on Google
Cloud Platform.

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:01.340
Thank you very much for coming.

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:03.024
And this was it.

00:20:03.024 --> 00:20:05.340
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:06.930
Thank you, guys.

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:08.550
Thank you, James.

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:11.090
JAMES BROOK: Thank you, Hygo.

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:14.830
So, I know many of you are
probably getting hungry.

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:17.180
And it's going to get a little
bit nerdy from this point

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:17.680
onwards.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:20.140
So, try to keep your brains
running for a little bit

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:22.030
longer.

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:24.970
So let's talk about
using capacity

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:26.870
efficiently for a moment.

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:28.810
So many people in
the room might be

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:33.040
familiar with the feeling of
having a few processes, say,

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:35.470
running on a VM or a few VMs.

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:38.940
And it doesn't feel logical to
put some other processes there,

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:40.373
so you leave it like that.

00:20:40.373 --> 00:20:42.580
But the VM is largely idling.

00:20:42.580 --> 00:20:44.620
The machine is not
using all of the memory

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:46.780
and CPU available to it.

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:51.160
Well the Kubernetes Scheduler is
really good at playing Tetris.

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:53.050
I think that's a good
way to think about it.

00:20:53.050 --> 00:20:56.050
It knows how to pack
those containers

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:58.560
into the available capacity
really efficiently.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.310
But I've heard it said
that you could even

00:21:00.310 --> 00:21:03.460
quadruple the effective
capacity of your data center

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:05.150
in this way.

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:07.190
But for some people
that's not enough.

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:11.410
So, let's talk about how you
could run a 5,000 node cluster.

00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:13.690
So maybe there's someone
in the room who would like

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:15.250
to run a 5,000 node cluster.

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:16.690
I've definitely
got colleagues who

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:20.560
would be interested in
talking to you afterwards.

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:25.270
So if you run a
5,000 node cluster,

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:27.370
and you used our 64
core machines which

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:29.530
have launched quite
recently, I think

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:34.570
you would have 233,600,000
core hours per month.

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:37.784
And you'd be paying about
$7 million a month for that.

00:21:37.784 --> 00:21:39.700
But once the sustained
use discount kicked in,

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:43.600
I think you'd get it
down to under $4 million.

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:45.804
But obviously not
everybody wants to do that.

00:21:45.804 --> 00:21:48.220
The good news, though, is that
because we operate clusters

00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:52.250
at scales like that, everyone
benefits in terms of latency.

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:55.210
So we have design
targets that are APIs.

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:58.330
The Kubernetes APIs should
respond in under one second

00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:00.520
and that pods should start
up within five seconds.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:06.580
So, if you--

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:08.765
Hygo mentioned federation.

00:22:08.765 --> 00:22:11.030
If you wanted to have the
ultimate high-availability

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:14.180
solution, you could run
a cluster in each region

00:22:14.180 --> 00:22:16.550
where you had customers
around the world

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:20.570
and get them to join a club,
which is called a federation,

00:22:20.570 --> 00:22:22.340
and get some sort of
centralized control

00:22:22.340 --> 00:22:24.106
over the components in it.

00:22:24.106 --> 00:22:26.480
And at the same time, you
could have a single global load

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.150
balancer in front of it,
which would automatically

00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:30.830
take care of routing
your clients'

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:33.750
requests to the nearest
healthy cluster in the world.

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:41.230
So as your clusters grow and
your operations teams grow,

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:42.730
you probably need
to think about how

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:45.680
you're going to control
access to all of these things.

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:49.940
All your eggs could be in
one basket now, otherwise.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:54.190
So in the past here we have
a blue team and a green team.

00:22:54.190 --> 00:22:56.890
If these people had access to
the cluster to change things,

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:57.980
then that was it.

00:22:57.980 --> 00:22:59.920
They could do what they liked.

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:02.290
Then along came
namespaces, which

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:04.780
gave us some form of
separation, but not

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:06.940
really enforced with teeth.

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:10.000
So, you could separate
out your concerns here.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:13.540
But you could just
do things like, say,

00:23:13.540 --> 00:23:15.640
the blue team will get
a certain amount of CPU

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:19.620
and memory and the green
team will get the rest.

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:25.240
But now with role-based access
control that can be enforced.

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:28.120
So if you have a user,
a group, or a service

00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:31.466
account and a resource that
you want to control access to--

00:23:31.466 --> 00:23:33.590
and a resource doesn't just
have to be a namespace.

00:23:33.590 --> 00:23:34.923
Here we have that as an example.

00:23:34.923 --> 00:23:36.250
It could be a pod.

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:39.470
Or it could even be, say,
read access to the logs

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:42.310
that that pod writes.

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:46.330
Now you can have a role, which
will play a part in connecting

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:47.350
those things together.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:52.090
So a role could be an
out-of-the-box role,

00:23:52.090 --> 00:23:56.020
so cluster admin ride the
way down to a custom role

00:23:56.020 --> 00:23:59.530
of somebody who could write
or read a particular resource

00:23:59.530 --> 00:24:01.980
or even a combination
of resources.

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:05.080
And you can connect them
together with a role binding,

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:07.240
giving you that fine
grained access control.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:10.930
Another thing is storage.

00:24:10.930 --> 00:24:13.720
So earlier we talked
about disconnecting

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:15.640
from the physical
world and just thinking

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:18.070
about our application and
our service-centric view

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:20.287
of the world.

00:24:20.287 --> 00:24:22.120
Unfortunately, storage
seems to take us back

00:24:22.120 --> 00:24:23.880
to that physical world.

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:25.990
And people may be
familiar with the days

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:28.180
when they had to
request storage,

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:30.220
say, their server
or their cluster

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:33.400
was running low and
panicking about the database,

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:36.070
causing it to
overflow and crash.

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:37.930
You would actually have
to request storage.

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:39.790
Maybe someone would
have to rack it.

00:24:39.790 --> 00:24:41.950
More recently, someone might--

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:44.410
separately still, an ops team,
perhaps-- might provision it

00:24:44.410 --> 00:24:46.310
with your cloud provider.

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:50.230
And then they would attach it
to a node or a VM, which still

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:52.827
doesn't quite fit in with
this service-centric view

00:24:52.827 --> 00:24:54.410
of the world we've
been talking about.

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:56.260
Because your pod
might one day be

00:24:56.260 --> 00:24:58.842
running on one node or
VM, and then the next day

00:24:58.842 --> 00:24:59.800
it might be on another.

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:02.720
So for a while we've had
persistent volume claims,

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:04.420
which give you a
means of associating

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:09.220
your storage after you've
provisioned it with a running

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:10.960
pod.

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:13.300
Wouldn't it be nice if
we could automate that?

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:15.490
Well now, we can do that
with dynamic provisioning.

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:19.030
So if a pod starts up and it
has a persistent volume claim

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:22.460
and it wants to have this
storage associated with it,

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:24.460
if it doesn't already
have access to the storage

00:25:24.460 --> 00:25:28.430
that storage can just
be created on the fly.

00:25:28.430 --> 00:25:31.390
And we also have something
called storage classes, which

00:25:31.390 --> 00:25:33.670
is basically a blueprint for
a certain type of storage

00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:39.550
you might have, say, different
cloud providers, or some SSDs.

00:25:39.550 --> 00:25:41.490
And you can effectively
give it a label

00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:43.670
and make an abstraction
there, and say, the SSD

00:25:43.670 --> 00:25:46.730
is your gold storage
or your fast storage.

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:49.944
So then all your persistent
volume claim has to do is say,

00:25:49.944 --> 00:25:52.360
I want this kind of storage,
and I want this amount of it.

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:54.310
And it will get sorted out.

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:57.010
So I think we're back in
the application-centric view

00:25:57.010 --> 00:25:59.120
of the world.

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:02.050
So we talked about all these
high-availability features.

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:05.150
Now we have some new
scheduling challenges as well.

00:26:05.150 --> 00:26:08.320
So you could run a cluster
across multiple availability

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:09.970
zones already.

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:14.560
And by default, the scheduler
will just put the containers

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:18.340
in the first free node that
it finds without much regard

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:20.430
to where things should be.

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:24.250
So you can have some
extra use cases.

00:26:24.250 --> 00:26:25.900
For instance, you
have a legacy service

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:30.190
running on a VM
in zone C. And you

00:26:30.190 --> 00:26:32.710
want to run some pods,
which particularly need

00:26:32.710 --> 00:26:34.609
to talk to that legacy service.

00:26:34.609 --> 00:26:36.400
I think you might like
them to be together,

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:37.983
because you want to
minimize and avoid

00:26:37.983 --> 00:26:40.210
that little bit of
latency that comes

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:43.777
with talking in between zones,
and some small costs as well.

00:26:43.777 --> 00:26:44.860
Or you might be like Hygo.

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:46.660
And you might have
a special node pool

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:49.210
with special hardware
for video encoding.

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:53.830
And you want your pods
to run in that node pool.

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:56.680
Well now we have extra control
over that with something

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:58.690
called node affinity.

00:26:58.690 --> 00:27:03.700
So in this we have our legacy
service running in zone C.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:07.070
And we want to schedule
a pod next to it.

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:09.820
And we can configure that
in something like this.

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:12.640
Now, I know this looks
like YAML on a slide,

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:14.140
and lunch is nearly here.

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:16.000
But its a bit like
poetry, I think.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:18.760
If you keep saying
require during scheduling,

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:23.620
ignore during execution, there's
something wonderful about that.

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:26.560
And I think if you think
about what that means,

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:28.532
it means that here
we've got something

00:27:28.532 --> 00:27:29.490
like a key and a value.

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:32.150
So we're saying that
our key is our failure

00:27:32.150 --> 00:27:34.330
domain, in this case a zone.

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:40.710
And we want our pod to be
landing in zone C here.

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:42.320
And we actually require that.

00:27:42.320 --> 00:27:46.700
So that is a strong requirement.

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:49.490
We could also say that we'd
prefer during scheduling,

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:51.430
ignore during execution.

00:27:51.430 --> 00:27:53.940
And unlike node selector, we
get these extra operators,

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:56.330
including some
exclusive operators that

00:27:56.330 --> 00:28:00.170
would let us repel workloads
from that particular key,

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:01.900
and some standard keys.

00:28:01.900 --> 00:28:05.780
So we have failure domains,
zone and region, host name,

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:09.170
and you can have custom things,
also preemptables, SSDs;

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:11.070
that sort of thing.

00:28:11.070 --> 00:28:14.110
So that gives us
some control there.

00:28:14.110 --> 00:28:16.970
But there are other use cases
that maybe node affinity

00:28:16.970 --> 00:28:18.220
doesn't help you with so well.

00:28:18.220 --> 00:28:21.500
So say, you have a front-end
service and a back-end service

00:28:21.500 --> 00:28:23.300
and you want them
to land together,

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:24.809
again to minimize latency.

00:28:24.809 --> 00:28:26.600
They're talking to each
other all the time,

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:28.308
but they're not actually
in the same pod.

00:28:28.308 --> 00:28:31.370
So these two processes ideally
are in the same availability

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:31.940
zone as well.

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:35.456
That's something that we call
north-south communication.

00:28:35.456 --> 00:28:36.830
And you don't want
to have to say

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:39.500
they have to be in zone
B or zone C. You just

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:42.170
want them to be in the
same availability zone.

00:28:42.170 --> 00:28:44.060
So that's something
that you can achieve

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:47.630
with inter-pod affinity.

00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:49.830
Here it is again, require
during scheduling,

00:28:49.830 --> 00:28:51.830
ignore during execution.

00:28:51.830 --> 00:28:54.200
So again, we have
a topology key.

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:57.620
In this case, we just
say that's the zone.

00:28:57.620 --> 00:29:00.080
And we have an
operator, which is in.

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:02.360
And we're saying
that our back end

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:05.330
should land in the same
zone as our front end.

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:07.160
We could say prefer
during scheduling,

00:29:07.160 --> 00:29:08.960
ignore during execution.

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:11.150
And in this case,
we could also say

00:29:11.150 --> 00:29:13.330
what happens at execution
is important as well.

00:29:13.330 --> 00:29:17.150
So say something changes
in the cluster topology,

00:29:17.150 --> 00:29:20.300
everything could move around.

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:22.550
So pods that were no
longer in the right place

00:29:22.550 --> 00:29:24.960
because something changed
would move to another node.

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:33.250
Now one more quite
techie thing, and that's

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:35.140
taints and tolerations.

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:39.220
So imagine you
have one node which

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:42.650
has got some special hardware
on it, and two regular nodes.

00:29:42.650 --> 00:29:44.570
So here we've got three nodes.

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:46.440
And one of them has a
couple of GPUs in it.

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:50.740
Say, for your use case,
Hygo, of video encoding.

00:29:50.740 --> 00:29:52.636
And here we've
got a regular pod,

00:29:52.636 --> 00:29:54.760
which is just going to do
some sort of regular job.

00:29:54.760 --> 00:29:56.880
It doesn't particularly
need a GPU.

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:59.810
And it comes along and
the cluster is bare,

00:29:59.810 --> 00:30:02.950
and it just gets scheduled
in the first node

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:04.280
that it comes to.

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:08.380
And that just happens to be
our GPU node, which is a shame.

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:12.340
Because next along
comes a GPU pod.

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:15.550
So by that I mean a pod
that's going to do some work

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:19.602
that would really benefit from
having access to that GPU.

00:30:19.602 --> 00:30:21.310
But we could have this
scenario now where

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:23.440
we've already scheduled
something on our GPU node.

00:30:23.440 --> 00:30:24.850
And it's basically full up.

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:26.240
There's no extra capacity there.

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:30.220
So either this GPU workload
won't get scheduled,

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:32.470
or it will have to
go somewhere else.

00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:35.170
And effectively, our GPU,
our expensive GPU hardware

00:30:35.170 --> 00:30:37.360
is wasted.

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:39.130
Well, we can solve
that with something

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:41.790
called taints and tolerations.

00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:43.690
So this is quite simple really.

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:47.960
We can label our GPU node
with something called a taint.

00:30:47.960 --> 00:30:51.016
So it's marked
that it has a GPU.

00:30:51.016 --> 00:30:54.430
And we say, we use this
expression, prefer no schedule,

00:30:54.430 --> 00:30:57.130
which just means it's
going to repel everything

00:30:57.130 --> 00:30:58.360
that comes its way.

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:01.450
So it's saying, I
don't want any pods

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:04.645
unless when the GPU
pod comes along,

00:31:04.645 --> 00:31:06.520
we've given it something
called a toleration.

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:12.100
So this pod can tolerate
running on a GPU node.

00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:15.430
So we'll get the happy case
where the regular pod has not

00:31:15.430 --> 00:31:18.220
been scheduled there because
it was repelled, because it

00:31:18.220 --> 00:31:20.380
did not have that toleration.

00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:23.500
But the pod with the
toleration for GPU,

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:26.070
the one the wants the
GPU, can take the slot.

00:31:29.150 --> 00:31:32.930
And now we end up in
this happy scenario.

00:31:32.930 --> 00:31:37.080
Now imagine, because we
say prefer no schedule,

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:40.150
imagine the case where our
regular pods have already

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:42.700
filled up all of the
available capacity,

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:45.130
and the only place left
to schedule something

00:31:45.130 --> 00:31:47.570
is on the pod which has the GPU.

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:49.755
Well, that can happen
because of that preference.

00:31:49.755 --> 00:31:53.540
So, it's a loose constraint.

00:31:53.540 --> 00:31:55.040
So, how many people
in the audience,

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:56.414
hold up your hand
if you've heard

00:31:56.414 --> 00:32:00.830
of this concept of an SRE,
a site reliability engineer.

00:32:00.830 --> 00:32:03.640
So that's something we
believe in a lot at Google.

00:32:03.640 --> 00:32:07.250
We literally wrote the book on
site reliability engineering.

00:32:07.250 --> 00:32:10.970
It's a major contribution we
made to the dev-ops culture,

00:32:10.970 --> 00:32:11.810
I would say.

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:13.640
Well, we want to
be your SRE too.

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:17.480
So Hygo talked about how
Google takes care of managing

00:32:17.480 --> 00:32:18.750
your control plane for you.

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:20.540
This takes away a
lot of the pain.

00:32:20.540 --> 00:32:23.990
So, routine ops-like
stuff you have to do,

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:27.050
like handling
backups, monitoring,

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:30.050
repairing the control plane
should something go wrong

00:32:30.050 --> 00:32:32.480
in the availability zone
or wherever it is that

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:33.930
the physical hardware--

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:35.430
in this case it's
Container Engine--

00:32:35.430 --> 00:32:37.210
so the availability zone.

00:32:37.210 --> 00:32:39.500
We take care of
keeping it healthy.

00:32:39.500 --> 00:32:42.750
And we can resize that control
plane for larger clusters.

00:32:42.750 --> 00:32:47.096
And we aim to keep it running
for you all of the time.

00:32:47.096 --> 00:32:49.220
And we also look after the
components on your node,

00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:50.845
so the Kubernetes components.

00:32:50.845 --> 00:32:54.080
So we give you things like
logging with stack driver,

00:32:54.080 --> 00:32:56.980
integrated with stack
driver monitoring metrics.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:32:59.180
We pre-install the
components needed

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:03.020
to act as a back end for
our global load balancer.

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:06.200
And now with 1.6, we can
manage your nodes too.

00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:09.290
So we can handle
operating system updates,

00:33:09.290 --> 00:33:13.881
security patches, Kubernetes
upgrades seamlessly.

00:33:13.881 --> 00:33:15.380
And if there's a
problem, if there's

00:33:15.380 --> 00:33:18.050
a problem with the kernel or
the VM, or it's out of memory,

00:33:18.050 --> 00:33:19.760
or something else
is detected; we

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:23.190
can also drain the pods that
are running on there, if we can,

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:26.840
and restart that node and bring
up another one to replace it.

00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:29.770
So you don't have to think
about that kind of thing.

00:33:29.770 --> 00:33:31.710
And so that you can keep
track of all of that,

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:32.834
we have a full audit trail.

00:33:32.834 --> 00:33:35.840
So you can tell whether
someone from your team

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:40.440
or the robot that took that
actions on your behalf.

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:43.050
So what's next?

00:33:43.050 --> 00:33:45.950
Well, in 1.7 we've got
another release coming.

00:33:45.950 --> 00:33:47.690
They come every three months.

00:33:47.690 --> 00:33:51.830
And they're pretty much bang
on time all of the time.

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:54.820
We're going to have better
support for GPUs, which is now

00:33:54.820 --> 00:33:58.140
a feature you can have on Google
Compute Engine and Container

00:33:58.140 --> 00:33:59.630
Engine.

00:33:59.630 --> 00:34:02.240
We're going to do more to
keep your secrets secret.

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:04.580
So you can integrate
with something

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:07.980
like Google Certificate
Manager or HashiCorp Vault

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:10.670
to store the secrets
that your services might

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:14.870
use to talk to each other or
things in the outside world.

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:17.190
We're going to have IP
virtual switch load balancing.

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:20.840
So that's a bit faster than
the existing built-in load

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:22.740
balancing using IP tables.

00:34:22.740 --> 00:34:25.820
We're going to make it easier
for add-ons to integrate

00:34:25.820 --> 00:34:28.219
with the core Kubernetes API.

00:34:28.219 --> 00:34:30.440
And it's going to be
possible to upgrade

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:35.929
the components of Kubernetes on
a running cluster more easily.

00:34:35.929 --> 00:34:37.130
And this is the thing--

00:34:37.130 --> 00:34:38.989
Matt mentioned it
earlier-- the thing

00:34:38.989 --> 00:34:40.790
that I'm most excited
about at the moment.

00:34:40.790 --> 00:34:42.748
Because I'm really
interested in microservices.

00:34:42.748 --> 00:34:45.300
And there are still
challenges with microservices.

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:47.210
Say, you hear the
story all the time

00:34:47.210 --> 00:34:51.470
about how people are
breaking their monoliths down

00:34:51.470 --> 00:34:52.610
into microservices.

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:54.710
But it does bring
some complexity.

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:57.950
Most successful microservices
stories started in that way,

00:34:57.950 --> 00:35:00.830
breaking down some existing
thing into a monolith.

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:03.590
And everyone or every
developer at least,

00:35:03.590 --> 00:35:07.640
is probably familiar with
dealing with concerns like, how

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:10.020
do I handle an HTTP failure.

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:14.330
How do I do HTTP
retries and exponential

00:35:14.330 --> 00:35:16.440
back-off, and that
kind of thing,

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:19.110
perhaps in five languages?

00:35:19.110 --> 00:35:23.300
How do I handle rate limiting
or internal load balancing in

00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:24.380
between my services?

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:27.120
How do those services
really discover each other?

00:35:27.120 --> 00:35:29.750
People are introducing all
sorts of additional frameworks

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:33.436
for service discovery.

00:35:33.436 --> 00:35:35.060
Why should my
application have to think

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:37.940
about its own encryption,
when that could just

00:35:37.940 --> 00:35:41.070
be handled by something at the
network level or service mesh?

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:43.230
And Istio brings you
all of those things.

00:35:43.230 --> 00:35:47.030
So it's something that's
completely open source.

00:35:47.030 --> 00:35:51.440
It's been designed to run on
any platform, any environment.

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:54.350
But first of all, it's
running on Kubernetes.

00:35:54.350 --> 00:35:56.000
It's an effort
from the community.

00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:58.490
So Google is obviously involved.

00:35:58.490 --> 00:36:02.750
IBM and Lyft brought their Envoy
proxy, which is a super-fast--

00:36:02.750 --> 00:36:07.430
it was already open source C++
Layer 7 proxy load balancer,

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:11.750
which automatically gets
injected next to your pods,

00:36:11.750 --> 00:36:14.360
picks up the traffic, and
can handle all kinds of great

00:36:14.360 --> 00:36:16.600
things like the things
I mentioned before,

00:36:16.600 --> 00:36:21.410
or even canary releasing, or
directing traffic that comes

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:24.170
from a particular person to a
particular back end or version

00:36:24.170 --> 00:36:25.760
of your service.

00:36:25.760 --> 00:36:27.170
And you can try it out today.

00:36:27.170 --> 00:36:30.440
So I think with just a couple
of commands you could install it

00:36:30.440 --> 00:36:34.857
in a Kubernetes cluster running
locally or on Container Engine.

00:36:34.857 --> 00:36:36.940
And there are some great
tutorials on the website.

00:36:36.940 --> 00:36:38.930
If you go to the
tasks there, you

00:36:38.930 --> 00:36:40.940
can get running with
canary releasing

00:36:40.940 --> 00:36:44.360
and use a specific sort of A/B
testing and things like that,

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:45.500
more or less straight away.

00:36:45.500 --> 00:36:49.010
So please have a
look at Istio.io.

00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:51.590
And if you're not already
playing with a Kubernetes

00:36:51.590 --> 00:36:54.310
cluster, you can do
that on your laptop.

00:36:54.310 --> 00:36:55.810
So you could try
all of the features

00:36:55.810 --> 00:36:59.170
I've talked about today by
installing MINICUBE, which is

00:36:59.170 --> 00:37:01.330
represented by the first logo.

00:37:01.330 --> 00:37:03.310
It's one command to
install it on a laptop.

00:37:03.310 --> 00:37:07.390
And you have a running single
node, obviously a cluster.

00:37:07.390 --> 00:37:09.670
Or you could take advantage
of Google Container Engine

00:37:09.670 --> 00:37:12.310
and our free pricing tier.

00:37:12.310 --> 00:37:16.750
So you get $300 a month, and you
could run a multi-node cluster

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:19.450
for quite a while on
Google Container Engine.

00:37:19.450 --> 00:37:23.020
And try out all of these things
like multi-availability zones.

00:37:23.020 --> 00:37:24.520
And have a look at
Istio, because I

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:26.620
think it's really cool.

00:37:26.620 --> 00:37:28.420
I'll be down here at
the front immediately

00:37:28.420 --> 00:37:29.680
afterwards, and then
out in the hall.

00:37:29.680 --> 00:37:31.971
So if you have any questions,
please just come up to me

00:37:31.971 --> 00:37:32.600
and ask.

00:37:32.600 --> 00:37:33.880
And thank you very much.

00:37:33.880 --> 00:37:36.330
[APPLAUSE]

